  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ミラクルフルーツ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%9F%E3%83%A9%E3%82%AF%E3%83%AB%E3%83%95%E3%83%AB%E3%83%BC%E3%83%84",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109582748,
      "text": "ミラクルフルーツ は、西アフリカ原産のアカテツ科の果物。果実自体は甘くないが、次に食べた物を甘く感じさせる特徴を持つ。ミラクルベリー とも呼ばれる。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Synsepalum dulcificum」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synsepalum_dulcificum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1369408784,
      "text": "Synsepalum dulcificum is a plant in the Sapotaceae family, native to tropical Africa. It is known for its berry that, when eaten, causes sour foods subsequently consumed to taste sweet. Common names for this species and its berry include miracle fruit, miracle berry, miraculous berry, sweet berry, and in West Africa, where the species originates, àgbáyun, taami, asaa, and ledidi."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Synsepalum dulcificum」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synsepalum_dulcificum#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1369408784,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "The plant grows best in soils with a pH as low as 4.5 to 5.8, in an environment free from frost and in partial shade with high humidity. It is tolerant of drought, full sunshine, and slopes.\nThe seeds need 14 to 21 days to germinate. A spacing of 4 m (13 ft) between plants is suggested.\nThe plants first bear fruit after growing about 3–4 years, and produce two crops per year, after the end of the rainy season. This evergreen plant produces small, red berries, while white flowers are produced for many months of the year.\nIn Africa, leaves are attacked by lepidopterous larvae, and fruits are infested with larvae of fruit flies. The fungus Rigidoporus microporus has been found on this plant.\nTransgenic tomato plants have been developed in research projects that produce miraculin.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
